               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate/>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#i"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_4">Canterbury, Kent.
                        Benedictine cathedral priory of Holy Trinity or Christ Church.
                        </orgName><p>At fol. i, two inscriptions: the first being 'Bibblia magistri
                        Thome Trussell [space] Eccl[es]ie xpi cantuarie' (s. xiv) and the later
                        'Bibblia dompni Will[el]mi Lighfyld monachi eccl[esi]e x. cant.' (s.
                        xv/xvi). All but the first four words of the earlier inscription have been
                        erased but legible under U.V. light.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Magister Thomas Trussel, Middlesex (1331) and
                     Northamptonshire (1344). </provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">For William Litchfield (1513), see Searle, p.
                     194.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">'Liber dompni William Cockes clerici' (fol.
                     i, s.xvi). </provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Henry Munster (s.xix). </provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">J. Poyner. </provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Sold at Sotheby's, 12 December 1967, lot 19
                     to Sanders, Oxford for £850, from whom it was bought by Hon. C. A.
                     Lennox-Boyd.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Sold at Christie's, 9 December 1981, lot 231,
                     to Heribert Tenschert, Rotthalmünster, from whom it was bought by Martin
                     Schøyen. It was no. 15 in his collection.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Sold at Dreweatts, 10 July 2018, lot 81 (as
                     'The Canterbury "Trussel" Bible'), to Canterbury Cathedral.</provenance>
               </history>
            </msDesc>
